 ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 44, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2026, "rank": 183, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 37, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "best-companies-southeast-asia", "title": "Fortune 100 Best Companies To Work For \u2013 Southeast Asia", "year": 2025, "rank": 71, "itemDescription": "<p>3M ranks No. 71 on the 2025 Best Companies to Work For Southeast Asia.</p>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 174, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 15, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 489, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--chemicals", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Chemicals", "year": 2024, "rank": 2, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 134, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 44, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 44, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 30, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 443, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 77, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 116, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": 29, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 402, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "modern-board-25", "title": "Fortune Modern Board 25", "year": 2022, "rank": 5, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The company founded as the Minnesota Mining and Manufacturing Company makes the list with an impressive and diverse group of directors. 3M\u2019s board scored highly in gender equality, senior independent nonexecutive director representation, and board expertise. While recent results for the company have been impacted by supply-chain constraints and rising inflation, long-term prospects look good.\u00a0</span><br />\r\n<br />\r\n<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Patricia A. Woertz, former chairman and CEO of Archer Daniels Midland, is one of six 3M board members (out of 13 total) to have held a CEO post. The others include Gregory R. Page (Cargill), Muhtar Kent (Coca-Cola), Herbert L. Henkel (Ingersoll-Rand), Michael L. Eskew (UPS), and David B. Dillon (Kroger). 3M CEO Michael Roman also serves as the board\u2019s chairman. Dow CEO James R. Fitterling is the other currently active CEO on the board.</span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0</span><br />\r\n<br />\r\n<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Roman has </span><a href=\"https://fortune.com/2021/03/09/the-pandemic-was-an-awakening-for-3m-ceo-daily/\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">called the pandemic</span></a><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> an \u201cawakening\u201d for 3M. In the aftermath of George Floyd\u2019s murder, Roman told </span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fortune </span></i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">in 2021, the Minneapolis-based company increased its focus on the well-being of employees and the economic health of the Twin Cities area. He said that business leaders can \u201cuse the same lessons and abilities you have built in business success and apply them more broadly to whatever is most important to your stakeholders\u2014and that includes your employees.\u201d</span><br />\r\n<br />\r\n<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The former CFOs of Microsoft and Accenture are 3M directors, as is Dambisa Moyo, former global economist at Goldman Sachs and director at Chevron and Cond\u00e9 Nast. 3M\u2019s board also includes Susan Kereere, the head of global business solutions at Fiserv, and Thomas K. Brown, the former group vice president of global purchasing at Ford. These two offer the perspective of the company\u2019s customers, an increasingly important voice to have on any corporate board.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 102, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": 24, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 382, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Conglomerate 3M weathered 2020 thanks to its diverse slate of businesses. While sales in many segments tumbled because of the pandemic, they rose in others. In the positive column were home improvement products (credit all those renovations during lockdown); personal safety items (production of N95 respirators tripled); and filters for biotech labs (as pharma companies pushed to create COVID-19 vaccines). But some of 3M\u2019s other big businesses suffered, including industrial products and office supplies. That\u2019s partly because corporate customers slowed their manufacturing and consumers switched to remote work and therefore didn\u2019t need as many Post-it notes. The result: flat revenue of $32.2 billion in 2020 and profits of $5.38 billion, up nearly 18%.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:12}\">Conglomerate 3M weathered 2020 thanks to its diverse slate of businesses. While sales in many segments tumbled because of the pandemic, they rose in others. In the positive column were home improvement products (credit all those renovations during lockdown); personal safety items (production of N95 respirators tripled); and filters for biotech labs (as pharma companies pushed to create COVID-19 vaccines). But some of 3M\u2019s other big businesses suffered, including industrial products and office supplies. That\u2019s partly because corporate customers slowed their manufacturing and consumers switched to remote work and therefore didn\u2019t need as many Post-it notes. The result: flat revenue of $32.2 billion in 2020 and profits of $5.38 billion, up nearly 18%.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-best-workplaces", "title": "World\u2019s 25 Best Workplaces", "year": 2021, "rank": 6, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 96, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Conglomerate 3M weathered 2020 thanks to its diverse slate of businesses. While sales in many segments tumbled because of the pandemic, they rose in others. In the positive column were home improvement products (credit all those renovations during lockdown); personal safety items (production of N95 respirators tripled); and filters for biotech labs (as pharma companies pushed to create COVID-19 vaccines). But some of 3M\u2019s other big businesses suffered, including industrial products and office supplies. That\u2019s partly because corporate customers slowed their manufacturing and consumers switched to remote work and therefore didn\u2019t need as many Post-it notes. The result: flat revenue of $32.2 billion in 2020 and profits of $5.38 billion, up nearly 18%.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:12}\">Conglomerate 3M weathered 2020 thanks to its diverse slate of businesses. While sales in many segments tumbled because of the pandemic, they rose in others. In the positive column were home improvement products (credit all those renovations during lockdown); personal safety items (production of N95 respirators tripled); and filters for biotech labs (as pharma companies pushed to create COVID-19 vaccines). But some of 3M\u2019s other big businesses suffered, including industrial products and office supplies. That\u2019s partly because corporate customers slowed their manufacturing and consumers switched to remote work and therefore didn\u2019t need as many Post-it notes. The result: flat revenue of $32.2 billion in 2020 and profits of $5.38 billion, up nearly 18%.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": 29, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 389, "itemDescription": "Based in St. Paul, Minn, and with corporate operations in 70 countries, 3M makes a wide range of products covering areas from health care to office supplies. The company has been in spotlight throughout the coronavirus pandemic, ramping up its production of N95 respirator masks and partnering with MIT in an attempt to develop a rapid COVID-19 test."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 103, "itemDescription": "The St. Paul, Minn.-based company, with corporate operations in 70 countries, makes a wide range of products covering areas from health care to office supplies."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": 27, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "ye